  <!-- EXECUTIVE SUMMARY -->
  <section class="sheet" id="summary">
    <h2>Executive summary</h2>
    <p>Reconnaissance is the first stage of a security test. Using only public information, six tools were run against networkwalks.com. Together they show who registered the domain, where it is hosted, what software it runs, and that a web application firewall sits in front of it.</p>
    <div class="tablewrap"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Finding</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Registrar</th><td>GoDaddy.com, LLC (domain created 6 Nov 2019, expires 6 Nov 2027)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Name servers</th><td>NS6135.HOSTGATOR.COM and NS6136.HOSTGATOR.COM, so the host is HostGator</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Server IP</th><td>192.232.216.135 (United States)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Web server</th><td>Apache, with nginx caching in front (x-nginx-cache header)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>CMS</th><td>WordPress 7.1.4 with WordPress Download Manager 3.3.76</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Front-end</th><td>Bootstrap 7.1.4, jQuery 3.7.1, Google Tag Manager</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Exposed contact</th><td>info@networkwalks.com</td></tr>
        <tr><th>WAF</th><td>ModSecurity (SpiderLabs)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Mail server</th><td>mail.networkwalks.com at 192.232.216.135 (same IP as the web server)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>DNS software</th><td>BIND 9.16.23-RH on both HostGator name servers (version visible)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>SPF policy</th><td>v=spf1 +a +mx +ip4:50.87.144.87 +include:websitewelcome.com ~all (soft fail)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>DNSSEC</th><td>Unsigned (no DNSSEC answer from dnsrecon either)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <h3>Overall assessment</h3>
    <p>The site discloses a normal amount of public information for a WordPress site on shared hosting. The most useful items for an attacker are the exact CMS and plugin versions, the server IP, and the REST API link in the response headers. The ModSecurity WAF and the HTTPS redirect with Secure and HttpOnly cookies are good defensive signs. Keeping WordPress and the plugin patched is the most important follow-up. The DNS records add a visible BIND version and a soft-fail SPF policy, and the web and mail services share one server IP.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- TASK 1 -->
  <section class="sheet" id="task1">
    <h2>Task 1: Domain registration details (whois)</h2>
    <p class="objective"><b>Objective:</b> Query the public registration record to learn who registered the domain, when, and its name servers.</p>
    <h3>Command run</h3>
    <div class="cmd"><code><span class="p">$</span><span class="t">whois networkwalks.com</span></code><button type="button" data-copy>Copy</button></div>
    <figure><div class="shot"><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="Terminal screenshot of the whois output for networkwalks.com"></div><figcaption>Figure 1: whois output</figcaption></figure>
    <h3>Key findings</h3>
    <div class="tablewrap"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Domain</th><td>NETWORKWALKS.COM</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Registrar</th><td>GoDaddy.com, LLC (IANA ID 146)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Created</th><td>2019-11-06</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Last updated</th><td>2025-11-12</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Expires</th><td>2027-11-06</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Name servers</th><td>NS6135.HOSTGATOR.COM, NS6136.HOSTGATOR.COM</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Status flags</th><td>clientDeleteProhibited, clientRenewProhibited, clientTransferProhibited, clientUpdateProhibited</td></tr>
        <tr><th>DNSSEC</th><td>unsigned</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Abuse contact</th><td>abuse@godaddy.com, 480-624-2505</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <h3>What this tells us</h3>
    <div class="stack">
      <p>The domain is about seven years old and paid up until November 2027. All four client lock flags are set, which makes accidental or unauthorised transfer and deletion harder.</p>
      <p>The name servers belong to HostGator, so the hosting provider is identified straight away even though the registrar is GoDaddy. Registrant personal details are hidden by privacy protection, which is good practice.</p>
    </div>
    <h3>Observation from the run</h3>
    <p>The first attempt returned a name resolution failure for whois.verisign-grs.com. This was a local DNS problem on the Kali VM, not a problem with the target. The command succeeded when re-run.</p>
    <h3>How an attacker could use it</h3>
    <p>Registration and expiry dates help in planning and in social engineering, for example a fake renewal notice just before expiry. Knowing the host lets an attacker research that provider's known weaknesses.</p>
    <h3>Defensive recommendations</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Keep registrar privacy protection on and the client lock flags enabled.</li>
      <li>Turn on auto-renew and monitor the expiry date to avoid domain hijacking after a lapse.</li>
      <li>Enable DNSSEC if the DNS host supports it, since the zone is currently unsigned.</li>
      <li>Use a registrar account with strong, unique credentials and multi-factor authentication.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- TASK 2 -->
  <section class="sheet" id="task2">
    <h2>Task 2: Web technology fingerprint (whatweb)</h2>
    <p class="objective"><b>Objective:</b> Identify the web server, CMS, plugins, frameworks and IP address of the site.</p>
    <h3>Command run</h3>
    <div class="cmd"><code><span class="p">$</span><span class="t">whatweb networkwalks.com</span></code><button type="button" data-copy>Copy</button></div>
    <figure><div class="shot"><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="Terminal screenshot of the whatweb output for networkwalks.com"></div><figcaption>Figure 2: whatweb output</figcaption></figure>
    <h3>Key findings</h3>
    <div class="tablewrap"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>HTTP behaviour</th><td>http:// returns 301 Moved Permanently to https://networkwalks.com/</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Final response</th><td>200 OK, page title: Networkwalks Academy</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Web server</th><td>Apache</td></tr>
        <tr><th>CMS</th><td>WordPress 7.1.4 (meta generator tag)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Plugin</th><td>WordPress Download Manager 3.3.76</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Libraries</th><td>Bootstrap 7.1.4, jQuery 3.7.1, HTML5, Google Tag Manager</td></tr>
        <tr><th>IP and country</th><td>192.232.216.135, United States</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Email exposed</th><td>info@networkwalks.com</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Cookie</th><td>__wpdm_client (HttpOnly)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Uncommon headers</th><td>permissions-policy, x-redirect-by, x-endurance-cache-level, x-nginx-cache, referrer-policy</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <h3>What this tells us</h3>
    <div class="stack">
      <p>whatweb confirmed the site runs WordPress with a download-management plugin, and it printed exact version numbers. The 301 redirect shows HTTP is forced to HTTPS, which is correct.</p>
      <p>The x-endurance-cache-level and x-nginx-cache headers point to a caching layer typical of HostGator managed WordPress hosting, which matches the whois finding.</p>
    </div>
    <h3>Observation from the run</h3>
    <p>Two attempts first failed with 'no address for networkwalks.com', and the final https request ended with 'execution expired'. Both were caused by unreliable DNS and a timeout on the VM. The fingerprint above comes from the successful run.</p>
    <h3>How an attacker could use it</h3>
    <p>Exact versions let an attacker search public vulnerability databases such as CVE lists and WPScan for known flaws in that WordPress release or the Download Manager plugin. The exposed email address can be used for phishing or spam.</p>
    <h3>Defensive recommendations</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Keep WordPress core and every plugin on the latest version, and remove plugins that are not needed.</li>
      <li>Hide the generator meta tag and version strings (a security plugin or a small filter can do this).</li>
      <li>Replace the public email with a contact form, or use an alias that is monitored for phishing.</li>
      <li>Review how WordPress Download Manager protects files and require login for sensitive downloads.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- TASK 3 -->
  <section class="sheet" id="task3">
    <h2>Task 3: DNS resolution (nslookup)</h2>
    <p class="objective"><b>Objective:</b> Resolve the domain name to its IP address using DNS.</p>
    <h3>Command run</h3>
    <div class="cmd"><code><span class="p">$</span><span class="t">nslookup networkwalks.com</span></code><button type="button" data-copy>Copy</button></div>
    <figure><div class="shot"><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="Terminal screenshot of the nslookup output for networkwalks.com"></div><figcaption>Figure 3: nslookup output</figcaption></figure>
    <h3>Key findings</h3>
    <div class="tablewrap"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Resolver used</th><td>8.8.8.8 (Google Public DNS), port 53</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Answer type</th><td>Non-authoritative answer</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Name</th><td>networkwalks.com</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Address</th><td>192.232.216.135</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Local errors</th><td>Repeated 'communications error to 10.0.0.1#53: connection refused'</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <h3>What this tells us</h3>
    <div class="stack">
      <p>The domain resolves to a single IPv4 address, 192.232.216.135. This is the same IP reported by whatweb, so the findings agree. The answer is non-authoritative because it came from Google's cache, not from the HostGator name servers.</p>
      <p>The warnings about 10.0.0.1 show the VM's default resolver (the local router or gateway) refused DNS queries. nslookup then fell back to 8.8.8.8, which explains why earlier whois and whatweb attempts failed.</p>
    </div>
    <h3>Observation from the run</h3>
    <p>The connection refused messages are a local configuration problem, not a finding about the target.</p>
    <h3>How an attacker could use it</h3>
    <p>With the IP, an attacker can target the server directly, check for other sites on the same shared host, and plan later scanning. The IP is public by design, so the risk is moderate.</p>
    <h3>Defensive recommendations</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Keep the origin server behind a firewall that only exposes ports that are needed.</li>
      <li>Consider a reverse proxy or CDN so that the origin IP is less exposed.</li>
      <li>Lab tip: fix the VM resolver (for example set 8.8.8.8 or 1.1.1.1 in /etc/resolv.conf or the network settings) so tools stop failing with name resolution errors.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- TASK 4 -->
  <section class="sheet" id="task4">
    <h2>Task 4: HTTP response headers (curl -I)</h2>
    <p class="objective"><b>Objective:</b> Read the HTTP response headers to see the server banner, status, cookies and redirects.</p>
    <h3>Command run</h3>
    <div class="cmd"><code><span class="p">$</span><span class="t">curl -I https://networkwalks.com</span></code><button type="button" data-copy>Copy</button></div>
    <figure><div class="shot"><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="Terminal screenshot of the curl header output for networkwalks.com"></div><figcaption>Figure 4: curl -I output</figcaption></figure>
    <h3>Key findings</h3>
    <div class="tablewrap"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Protocol and status</th><td>HTTP/2 200</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Server</th><td>Apache</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Content type</th><td>text/html; charset=UTF-8</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Date header</th><td>Tue, 10 Nov 2026 10:10:55 GMT</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Cache headers</th><td>x-nginx-cache: WordPress, x-endurance-cache-level: 0</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Referrer policy</th><td>no-referrer-when-downgrade</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Link header</th><td>REST API at https://networkwalks.com/wp-json/ and page JSON at /wp-json/wp/v2/pages/53</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Cookie</th><td>__wpdm_client=..., path=/, domain=networkwalks.com, Secure, HttpOnly</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Permissions-Policy</th><td>private-state-token-redemption and issuance allowed for Google, gstatic, recaptcha, Cloudflare challenges, hCaptcha</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <h3>What this tells us</h3>
    <div class="stack">
      <p>The site serves over HTTP/2 with a valid response. The cookie is flagged Secure and HttpOnly, so it cannot be read by JavaScript or sent over plain HTTP, which is good.</p>
      <p>The Link header advertises the WordPress REST API. The permissions policy lists captcha providers, which suggests the site uses reCAPTCHA or hCaptcha on its forms. Missing from the response are several modern security headers.</p>
    </div>
    <h3>Observation from the run</h3>
    <p>The date header (10 Nov 2026) shows the server clock is correct and matches the day of testing.</p>
    <h3>How an attacker could use it</h3>
    <p>Headers confirm the stack (Apache plus WordPress) without loading the page, and they reveal the REST API, which can allow user enumeration at /wp-json/wp/v2/users if it is not restricted. The absence of Strict-Transport-Security, Content-Security-Policy and X-Frame-Options makes downgrade, clickjacking and script-injection attacks easier.</p>
    <h3>Defensive recommendations</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Add Strict-Transport-Security, Content-Security-Policy, X-Content-Type-Options and X-Frame-Options headers.</li>
      <li>Restrict the REST API for anonymous users where it is not needed, in particular user listing.</li>
      <li>Hide the server banner and the Link header where possible.</li>
      <li>Use a stricter referrer policy such as strict-origin-when-cross-origin.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- TASK 5 -->
  <section class="sheet" id="task5">
    <h2>Task 5: Web Application Firewall detection (wafw00f)</h2>
    <p class="objective"><b>Objective:</b> Detect whether a Web Application Firewall is protecting the target site.</p>
    <h3>Command run</h3>
    <div class="cmd"><code><span class="p">$</span><span class="t">wafw00f networkwalks.com</span></code><button type="button" data-copy>Copy</button></div>
    <figure><div class="shot"><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="Terminal screenshot of wafw00f reporting ModSecurity for networkwalks.com"></div><figcaption>Figure 5: wafw00f output</figcaption></figure>
    <h3>Key findings</h3>
    <div class="tablewrap"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Tool version</th><td>WAFW00F v2.4.2</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Target checked</th><td>https://networkwalks.com</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Result</th><td>Behind ModSecurity (SpiderLabs) WAF</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Requests sent</th><td>2</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <h3>What this tells us</h3>
    <div class="stack">
      <p>wafw00f identified ModSecurity from SpiderLabs, an open-source WAF that is common on cPanel and HostGator hosting. It needed only two requests, so the footprint on the target was very small.</p>
      <p>A WAF means simple attack payloads such as basic SQL injection or XSS strings will likely be blocked or logged. It does not remove vulnerabilities in the application, it only filters some attacks.</p>
    </div>
    <h3>How an attacker could use it</h3>
    <p>Knowing the WAF vendor tells an attacker to slow down, use evasion techniques, or look for ways around the WAF such as hitting the origin IP directly. It also shapes the choice of tools in later phases.</p>
    <h3>Defensive recommendations</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Keep the ModSecurity rule set (for example OWASP Core Rule Set) updated and monitor its logs.</li>
      <li>Make sure the origin server only accepts traffic that passed through the WAF where possible.</li>
      <li>Do not rely on the WAF alone: patch WordPress and plugins regularly.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- TASK 6 -->
  <section class="sheet" id="task6">
    <h2>Task 6: DNS enumeration (dnsrecon)</h2>
    <p class="objective"><b>Objective:</b> Enumerate DNS records: name servers, mail servers, SPF, TXT and service (SRV) records.</p>
    <h3>Command run</h3>
    <div class="cmd"><code><span class="p">$</span><span class="t">dnsrecon -d networkwalks.com</span></code><button type="button" data-copy>Copy</button></div>
    <figure class="w1"><div class="shot"><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="Terminal screenshot of the dnsrecon output for networkwalks.com"></div><figcaption>Figure 6: dnsrecon output</figcaption></figure>
    <h3>Key findings</h3>
    <div class="tablewrap"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>SOA</th><td>ns6135.hostgator.com (50.87.144.87)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Name servers</th><td>ns6135.hostgator.com (50.87.144.87), ns6136.hostgator.com (192.232.216.131)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>DNS software</th><td>BIND version 9.16.23-RH reported by both name servers</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Mail (MX)</th><td>mail.networkwalks.com (192.232.216.135)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>A record</th><td>networkwalks.com resolves to 192.232.216.135</td></tr>
        <tr><th>TXT: SPF</th><td>v=spf1 +a +mx +ip4:50.87.144.87 +include:websitewelcome.com ~all</td></tr>
        <tr><th>TXT: verification</th><td>google-site-verification string present (Google domain ownership check)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>SRV records</th><td>_autodiscover._tcp.networkwalks.com points to cpanelemaildiscovery.cpanel.net on port 443, with 8 addresses in 184.94.203.x and 184.94.204.x</td></tr>
        <tr><th>DNSSEC</th><td>No answer for the DNSSEC query (not signed)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Summary line</th><td>8 records found, enumeration completed</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <h3>What this tells us</h3>
    <div class="stack">
      <p>dnsrecon confirms the HostGator name servers seen in whois and shows that the web server and the mail server share the same IP, 192.232.216.135. That is typical of shared cPanel hosting, and it means one compromised server could affect both the website and email.</p>
      <p>Both name servers report BIND 9.16.23-RH. The RH suffix points to a Red Hat based build. Exposing the exact version lets an attacker look for known BIND vulnerabilities for that release.</p>
      <p>The SPF record lists the host's own IP and the websitewelcome.com mail service. It ends with ~all, a soft fail, so mail from other senders may still be accepted by receivers. The SRV autodiscover records show the site uses cPanel email auto-configuration.</p>
    </div>
    <h3>Observation from the run</h3>
    <p>The first attempt ended with a timeout error ('please make sure you can reach the target DNS servers directly'), caused by the local DNS and network problem on the VM. The scan completed on a later run, on 11 Nov 2026 (timestamps show the VM's -0500 time zone). SRV addresses were read from the screenshot.</p>
    <h3>How an attacker could use it</h3>
    <p>The DNS footprint gives an attacker the full email and hosting layout. A soft-fail SPF policy makes email spoofing of the domain easier, for example fake messages that appear to come from info@networkwalks.com. The BIND version and cPanel records help them choose which hosting weaknesses to research.</p>
    <h3>Defensive recommendations</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Change SPF from ~all to -all once every legitimate sending service is listed, and add DKIM and DMARC records.</li>
      <li>Ask the DNS host (HostGator) to hide the BIND version string, since it is the host's server, not your own.</li>
      <li>Enable DNSSEC if the DNS provider supports it.</li>
      <li>Make sure zone transfers (AXFR) are refused for everyone except the secondary name servers.</li>
      <li>Consider separating mail and web onto different servers or services to limit the damage from one compromise.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- CONCLUSION -->
  <section class="sheet" id="conclusion">
    <h2>Conclusion and priority actions</h2>
    <p>The reconnaissance built a clear profile of networkwalks.com from public data alone: a GoDaddy-registered domain hosted on HostGator, served by Apache with caching, running WordPress 7.1.4 and WordPress Download Manager 3.3.76, and protected by a ModSecurity WAF. Nothing here shows a vulnerability by itself. The value is that these facts guide the scanning and testing in the next projects.</p>
    <div class="tablewrap"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Priority</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>1. Patch</th><td>Keep WordPress core and WordPress Download Manager fully up to date and remove unused plugins.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>2. Headers</th><td>Add HSTS, CSP, X-Frame-Options and X-Content-Type-Options.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>3. REST API</th><td>Restrict anonymous user enumeration through /wp-json/.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>4. Information leakage</th><td>Hide version strings and replace the public email with a form.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>5. DNS and email</th><td>Enable DNSSEC if supported, hide the BIND version, and move SPF from ~all to -all once all senders are listed.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>6. Lab setup</th><td>Fix the Kali VM DNS resolver so tools do not fail with name resolution errors.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p class="small">Liability note: this work was done for learning on a lab target. Testing any system without written permission from its owner is illegal.</p>
  </section>

